// Domain 4: Security and Compliance (16%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d4-01", d: 4,
  q: "A company requires IAM users to sign in with MFA before they can do anything other than manage their own MFA device. Some users sign in without MFA and can still stop EC2 instances.\n\nWhich IAM policy approach will enforce this requirement?",
  o: ["Attach a policy that denies all but MFA self-management actions when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is false.",
      "Attach a policy that allows all actions only when the aws:SecureTransport condition key is set to true.",
      "Turn on an account password policy that requires users to change their passwords every 30 days.",
      "Attach a policy that denies all actions whenever the aws:RequestedRegion condition key is not us-east-1."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Deny statement with a condition such as BoolIfExists aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent false blocks every action except the ones a user needs to set up MFA. After the user signs in with MFA, the condition no longer matches, and the user's other permissions apply.",
      "aws:SecureTransport checks whether a request used TLS. It has nothing to do with MFA.",
      "A password policy controls password strength and rotation. It does not require MFA.",
      "aws:RequestedRegion restricts Regions. It does not check whether the user signed in with MFA."] },

{ id: "d4-02", d: 4,
  q: "An application on an EC2 instance reads objects from an S3 bucket. The developers stored an IAM user's access keys in the application's configuration file. A security review flags this as a risk.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to remove the risk?",
  o: ["Attach an IAM role with read access to the bucket through an instance profile, and delete the keys.",
      "Encrypt the configuration file with AWS KMS so that the access keys cannot be read from the disk directly.",
      "Move the access keys into environment variables on the instance, and remove them from the file.",
      "Rotate the access keys every 90 days, and store the new keys in the same configuration file."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An instance profile gives the application temporary credentials that rotate automatically through the instance metadata service. The SDK picks them up with no keys in files, and the long-lived keys can be deleted.",
      "The application still has to decrypt and use long-lived keys, so the keys can still leak and never expire.",
      "Environment variables still hold long-lived keys that can leak through logs or process listings.",
      "Rotation limits how long a key is exposed, but the keys are still long-lived and stored on the instance."] },

{ id: "d4-03", d: 4,
  q: "A third-party monitoring vendor needs read-only access to a company's AWS account. The vendor serves many customers and calls the company's account from the vendor's own AWS account.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer grant access securely?",
  o: ["Create an IAM role that trusts the vendor's account, and require a unique external ID in the trust policy.",
      "Create an IAM user for the vendor with read-only permissions, and send the access keys by email.",
      "Share the company account's root credentials with the vendor so that it can create its own access.",
      "Add the vendor's account to the company's S3 bucket policies, and allow all read actions on all buckets."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A cross-account role lets the vendor use temporary credentials from its own account. Requiring a unique external ID in the trust policy (the sts:ExternalId condition) protects against the confused deputy problem, where another of the vendor's customers could trick the vendor into accessing your account.",
      "Long-lived access keys sent by email can leak and must be rotated by hand, and they are not needed for cross-account access.",
      "Root credentials must never be shared. They have unrestricted access to the account.",
      "Bucket policies cover only Amazon S3, and monitoring needs read access across many services."] },

{ id: "d4-04", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 25 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations and 2,000 employees in Microsoft Entra ID. Employees must sign in with their corporate credentials and get access to accounts based on their Entra ID groups. The company wants to stop using IAM users.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Connect IAM Identity Center to Entra ID with SAML and SCIM, and assign permission sets to groups.",
      "Create an IAM user in every account for each employee, and sync their passwords from Entra ID every night.",
      "Create a SAML identity provider and roles in each of the 25 accounts, and map groups in each one.",
      "Create one IAM user per team in the management account, and share the passwords with each team."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["IAM Identity Center provides single sign-on to all accounts in the organization. SAML federates sign-in with Entra ID, SCIM provisions users and groups automatically, and permission sets that are assigned to groups create the roles in each account.",
      "This keeps thousands of IAM users with long-lived credentials, which the company wants to stop.",
      "Per-account SAML federation works, but it must be built and maintained in 25 accounts, which is more overhead.",
      "Shared credentials break accountability and least privilege."] },

{ id: "d4-05", d: 4,
  q: "An S3 bucket contains sensitive data. The company requires that the bucket can be accessed only through a specific gateway VPC endpoint in the company's VPC, even by users who have IAM permissions to the bucket.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that denies all S3 actions when aws:SourceVpce does not equal the endpoint ID.",
      "Add a bucket policy that allows access only when aws:SourceIp matches the VPC's CIDR block.",
      "Attach a security group to the bucket that allows inbound HTTPS only from the gateway VPC endpoint.",
      "Add a route in the VPC route table that sends all traffic for the bucket to a NAT gateway."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Deny statement with a StringNotEquals condition on aws:SourceVpce blocks every request that does not arrive through that endpoint. An explicit deny overrides any allow, so even users with IAM permissions cannot reach the bucket from anywhere else.",
      "aws:SourceIp does not contain private VPC addresses for requests that arrive through a VPC endpoint, so this condition would not work as intended.",
      "S3 buckets do not have security groups.",
      "A NAT gateway sends traffic to the public S3 endpoint, which does not limit access to the VPC endpoint."] },

{ id: "d4-06", d: 4,
  q: "For data residency, a company must keep all workloads in eu-central-1 and eu-west-1. Users in member accounts must not be able to create resources in other Regions, but global services such as IAM and Route 53 must keep working.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Attach an SCP that denies requests to other Regions by using aws:RequestedRegion, with global services exempted.",
      "Attach an IAM policy to each IAM user and role that allows actions only in eu-central-1 and eu-west-1.",
      "Turn on AWS Config in all other Regions with a rule that deletes any new resources that it finds.",
      "Opt out of every other Region for each member account from the account settings in the management account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An SCP that denies aws:RequestedRegion values other than the two approved Regions sets a guardrail for every principal in member accounts. Exempting global services such as IAM, AWS Organizations, Route 53, CloudFront, and AWS Support keeps them working, because their requests go to us-east-1.",
      "IAM policies on each identity are easy to miss or change, and new roles would not be covered automatically.",
      "Deleting resources after they are created is reactive, and data might already be stored in the wrong Region.",
      "Only opt-in Regions can be disabled. Regions that are enabled by default cannot be turned off, so this cannot block them."] },

{ id: "d4-07", d: 4,
  q: "A company lets developers create IAM roles for their Lambda functions. The security team must make sure that any role a developer creates can never have more than a defined set of permissions, even if the developer attaches AdministratorAccess to it.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Require a permissions boundary on every role that developers create, enforced with an IAM condition key.",
      "Attach an SCP to the account that denies the developers access to the iam:CreateRole action.",
      "Turn on IAM Access Analyzer so that any role with too many permissions is deleted automatically every day.",
      "Ask developers to use only AWS managed policies when they attach policies to their new roles."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A permissions boundary sets the maximum permissions that identity-based policies can grant to a role. If the developers' own policy allows iam:CreateRole only when the iam:PermissionsBoundary condition matches the approved boundary, every role they create is capped, even with AdministratorAccess attached.",
      "Denying role creation stops developers from doing their work, which is not the goal.",
      "Access Analyzer reports findings. It does not delete roles or cap their permissions.",
      "AWS managed policies include AdministratorAccess, and a request to developers is not an enforced control."] },

{ id: "d4-08", d: 4,
  q: "Hundreds of projects share one AWS account. Engineers must be able to start and stop only the EC2 instances that belong to their own project. New projects are added every week, and the security team does not want to update IAM policies for each new project.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Tag instances and roles with Project, and allow actions only when the resource and principal Project tags match.",
      "Create an IAM policy for each project that lists the instance IDs of that project's instances, and update it weekly.",
      "Create a separate VPC for each project, and allow the engineers to manage instances only inside their own VPC.",
      "Create an IAM group for each project, and add a new group with its own policy every week for each new project."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attribute-based access control (ABAC) uses tags in policy conditions. One policy that compares aws:ResourceTag/Project with aws:PrincipalTag/Project works for every project, including new ones, as long as resources and roles are tagged correctly. Engineers must not be allowed to change the Project tag.",
      "Per-project policies with instance IDs must change whenever instances or projects change, which is the work the team wants to avoid.",
      "IAM does not easily scope EC2 start and stop actions by VPC, and a VPC for each project adds network overhead.",
      "A new group and policy every week is the manual work the team wants to avoid."] },

{ id: "d4-09", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 60 member accounts in AWS Organizations, and the security team must store and protect the root user credentials of each one. The team wants to remove root credentials from member accounts but still be able to perform root-only tasks, such as unlocking an S3 bucket policy that denies everyone.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on centralized root access, remove member root credentials, and run root-only tasks as privileged sessions.",
      "Turn on MFA for the root user of each member account, and keep all of the hardware MFA devices in a safe.",
      "Attach an SCP that denies all actions for the root user in member accounts, including the root-only tasks.",
      "Rotate the root user password of every member account every 30 days, and store it in AWS Secrets Manager."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Centralized root access management lets the management account or a delegated administrator remove root credentials from member accounts and block their recovery. Short-lived privileged root sessions can then perform root-only tasks, such as deleting a misconfigured bucket policy, without long-lived root credentials.",
      "MFA protects the root user, but the credentials still exist in every account and must still be stored and protected.",
      "Denying root actions would block the root-only tasks that the team still needs, and the credentials would still exist.",
      "Rotating passwords still leaves long-lived root credentials in every account."] },

{ id: "d4-10", d: 4,
  q: "An application role sometimes receives AccessDenied errors from AWS KMS when it reads encrypted objects from Amazon S3. A CloudOps engineer needs to find which KMS key, which API action, and which principal were involved in each failure.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Search CloudTrail for kms.amazonaws.com events with errorCode AccessDenied, and review their details.",
      "Turn on S3 server access logging, and wait for the next failure to see the error in the logs.",
      "Run the IAM policy simulator against every KMS key in the account with every possible KMS action.",
      "Turn on VPC Flow Logs for the application's subnets, and look for rejected traffic going to AWS KMS."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail records KMS API calls, including failed ones, with the errorCode, the action (such as Decrypt), the key ARN, and the userIdentity of the caller. CloudTrail Lake or CloudWatch Logs Insights can filter these events quickly.",
      "Server access logs show S3 requests, but they do not show the KMS call details or the key that denied access.",
      "Simulating every key and action is slow and would not show what actually failed.",
      "Flow logs show network traffic. AccessDenied is a permissions error, not a network problem."] },

{ id: "d4-11", d: 4,
  q: "Before attaching a new IAM policy to a production role, a CloudOps engineer wants to confirm that the role will be allowed to call s3:PutObject on one bucket and denied on all other buckets. The check must account for the SCPs and permissions boundary that apply to the role.\n\nWhich tool should the engineer use?",
  o: ["The IAM policy simulator",
      "AWS Trusted Advisor checks",
      "Amazon Inspector findings",
      "AWS Config rule evaluations"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The IAM policy simulator tests identity-based policies, permissions boundaries, resource-based policies, and SCPs against chosen actions and resources. It shows whether each request would be allowed or denied without making real requests.",
      "Trusted Advisor checks for best practices, such as unrestricted security groups. It does not evaluate specific policy decisions.",
      "Inspector finds software vulnerabilities and network exposure. It does not test IAM policies.",
      "Config evaluates resource configurations against rules. It does not simulate API authorization."] },

{ id: "d4-12", d: 4,
  q: "The security team wants to be notified whenever an S3 bucket, KMS key, IAM role, or Lambda function in any of the organization's accounts is shared with a principal outside the organization.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an organization external access analyzer in IAM Access Analyzer, and route findings to SNS with EventBridge.",
      "Write a Lambda function that reads every resource policy each hour and compares the principals with the organization.",
      "Turn on GuardDuty in every account, and alert on findings that show data being read from S3 buckets.",
      "Use Trusted Advisor to check bucket permissions weekly, and email the results to the security team."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An external access analyzer that uses the organization as its zone of trust analyzes resource-based policies and reports findings for resources shared outside the organization. Findings are sent to EventBridge, so a rule can notify the team through Amazon SNS.",
      "This is custom code that must understand complex policy logic for many resource types.",
      "GuardDuty detects threats from activity. It does not analyze resource policies for external sharing.",
      "The Trusted Advisor check covers only S3 bucket permissions, and a weekly email is not timely."] },

{ id: "d4-13", d: 4,
  q: "A company wants to find IAM roles that have not been used for 90 days, and permissions inside roles that are never used, so that it can remove them and enforce least privilege across all of its accounts.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an unused access analyzer in IAM Access Analyzer with a 90-day tracking period.",
      "Create an external access analyzer in IAM Access Analyzer with the account as the zone of trust.",
      "Search the CloudTrail event history in each account for roles that have no events in 90 days.",
      "Turn on AWS Config, and use a managed rule that deletes IAM roles that are older than 90 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An unused access analyzer reports unused roles, unused access keys and passwords, and unused service and action permissions, based on a tracking period that you choose. It can cover the whole organization and gives findings that you can act on.",
      "External access analyzers find resources shared outside the zone of trust. They do not report unused permissions.",
      "Event history keeps only 90 days of management events for each Region, and analyzing it by hand across all accounts is slow and incomplete.",
      "A role's age does not show whether it is used, and deleting roles automatically could break workloads."] },

{ id: "d4-14", d: 4,
  q: "A new application role has broad permissions. The application has run in a test account for 30 days with CloudTrail turned on. The team wants a least-privilege policy based on what the application actually used.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate a policy from the role's CloudTrail activity, and review it.",
      "Use the IAM policy simulator to generate a policy that allows every action the role has access to.",
      "Attach the ReadOnlyAccess managed policy, and add actions back when the application fails.",
      "Use AWS Config to record the role's permissions, and copy the recorded policy to production."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access Analyzer policy generation reviews the role's CloudTrail activity for a chosen period and creates a policy with the services and actions that the role actually used. The team reviews and refines the policy before attaching it.",
      "The simulator tests existing policies. It does not generate policies, and allowing every action is not least privilege.",
      "Fixing failures one at a time in production causes outages and slow progress.",
      "Config records the current broad policy, so copying it does not reduce the permissions."] },

{ id: "d4-15", d: 4,
  q: "A user in a member account has an IAM policy that allows ec2:RunInstances on all resources. When the user tries to launch a p4d.24xlarge instance, the request fails with an explicit deny, but launches of t3 instances work. No permissions boundary is attached to the user.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["An SCP that applies to the account denies ec2:RunInstances for certain instance types.",
      "The user's IAM policy is missing the ec2:DescribeInstanceTypes action for large types.",
      "The account has an On-Demand Instance quota that is too low for the p4d instance family.",
      "The p4d.24xlarge instance type requires the user to sign in with the AWS account root user."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An explicit deny that the user's own policy does not explain often comes from a service control policy. SCPs can deny actions with conditions such as ec2:InstanceType, and an explicit deny overrides the IAM allow. Decoding the authorization message shows which kind of policy denied the request.",
      "Describe permissions are not needed to launch a specific instance type, and a missing permission would cause an implicit deny, not an explicit deny.",
      "A quota problem returns a limit exceeded error, not an access denied error.",
      "Launching instances never requires the root user."] },

{ id: "d4-16", d: 4,
  q: "An EC2 API call fails with an error that includes an encoded authorization failure message. A CloudOps engineer needs to see which policy statement denied the request.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Run aws sts decode-authorization-message with the encoded message.",
      "Run aws iam simulate-custom-policy with the encoded message text.",
      "Base64 decode the message locally to read the policy statement.",
      "Paste the encoded message into the CloudTrail console to decode it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["STS DecodeAuthorizationMessage turns the encoded message into readable details about the request, including the matched statements and whether the deny was explicit. The caller needs the sts:DecodeAuthorizationMessage permission.",
      "The policy simulator tests policies that you give it. It does not decode authorization messages.",
      "The message is encrypted, not just encoded, so it cannot be read with a simple Base64 decode.",
      "CloudTrail records the failed call, but it does not decode this message."] },

{ id: "d4-17", d: 4,
  q: "Account A owns an S3 bucket whose objects are encrypted with a customer managed KMS key in account A. A role in account B has an IAM policy that allows s3:GetObject on the bucket, and the bucket policy allows the role. Downloads from account B still fail with AccessDenied.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Allow the account B role to use kms:Decrypt in the key policy, and allow kms:Decrypt in the role's IAM policy.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3 for new objects, and leave the existing objects encrypted as they are.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Ownership with the bucket owner preferred setting so that account B owns its objects.",
      "Add the account B role to the bucket's ACL with full control, in addition to the existing bucket policy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["To read objects encrypted with SSE-KMS, the caller needs permission to use the key. Across accounts, the key policy in account A must allow the account B role (or account B), and the role's IAM policy in account B must also allow kms:Decrypt on the key ARN.",
      "Existing objects stay encrypted with the KMS key, so reads of them would still fail.",
      "Object Ownership controls who owns uploaded objects. It does not grant KMS permissions.",
      "ACLs grant S3 permissions only, and S3 access is already allowed. The missing piece is the KMS key permission."] },

{ id: "d4-18", d: 4,
  q: "A company attaches an SCP to the organization root that denies stopping CloudTrail trails. During a test, a user in the management account can still stop a trail.\n\nWhy can the user stop the trail?",
  o: ["SCPs do not affect users and roles in the management account.",
      "SCPs apply only to the root users of the member accounts.",
      "SCPs take 24 hours to apply after they are attached to the root.",
      "SCPs apply only to resources created after the SCP is attached."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Service control policies never restrict the management account, even when they are attached to the organization root. Sensitive actions there must be controlled with IAM policies, and workloads should run in member accounts.",
      "SCPs apply to all IAM users and roles in member accounts, including the root user of those accounts.",
      "SCPs take effect quickly after they are attached. There is no 24-hour delay.",
      "SCPs restrict actions on all resources, no matter when the resources were created."] },

{ id: "d4-19", d: 4,
  q: "The security team wants to manage Amazon GuardDuty, AWS Security Hub CSPM, and IAM Access Analyzer for all accounts from a dedicated security account instead of the management account.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Register the security account as the delegated administrator for each service in Organizations.",
      "Move the security account to the root OU so that it inherits the management account's permissions.",
      "Create IAM users in the management account for the security team, and use them for each service.",
      "Share the management account's root credentials with the security team for these three services."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Many security services support a delegated administrator. Registering the security account as the delegated administrator lets it turn on and manage the service for all member accounts, so the security team does not need to work in the management account.",
      "OU placement does not grant administrative powers over other accounts.",
      "This keeps the team working in the management account, which AWS recommends limiting to essential tasks.",
      "Root credentials must never be shared."] },

{ id: "d4-20", d: 4,
  q: "A company is setting up a new multi-account environment. New accounts must be created with a standard baseline, central logging, and preventive and detective guardrails applied automatically. The company wants the LEAST custom work.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Set up AWS Control Tower, and create accounts through Account Factory in governed OUs.",
      "Create accounts with the Organizations API, and run a script that configures each new account.",
      "Create accounts by hand, and ask each account owner to follow a written security checklist.",
      "Create one account for every workload, and use IAM policies as the only guardrails."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Control Tower sets up a landing zone with log archive and audit accounts, and Account Factory creates new accounts with a standard baseline. Controls are applied to OUs as preventive SCPs, detective AWS Config rules, and proactive CloudFormation hooks.",
      "Scripts can work, but they are custom tooling to build and maintain.",
      "Manual setup and checklists are inconsistent, and they do not enforce guardrails.",
      "IAM policies alone can be changed by account administrators, and they give no organization-wide guardrails or central logging."] },

{ id: "d4-21", d: 4,
  q: "The finance team requires every new EC2 instance to have a CostCenter tag with one of five approved values. Launches that do not include a valid tag must be blocked in all accounts in the organization.\n\nWhich combination of actions will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a tag policy that defines CostCenter and its allowed values, with enforcement for EC2 instances.",
      "Create an SCP that denies ec2:RunInstances when aws:RequestTag/CostCenter is missing.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule that deletes instances without a CostCenter tag once a day.",
      "Ask each team to add the CostCenter tag in the console when they launch instances.",
      "Turn on cost allocation tags in the Billing console for the CostCenter tag key only."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Tag policies standardize tag keys and values across the organization. With enforcement turned on for a resource type, a request that sets the tag to a value that is not allowed is blocked.",
      "Tag policies do not block resources that are created without the tag. An SCP that denies ec2:RunInstances when the aws:RequestTag/CostCenter key is null makes the tag required at launch.",
      "Deleting instances after they launch is disruptive, and it does not block the launch.",
      "A request to teams is not enforced, and it does not cover the CLI or automation.",
      "Cost allocation tags make tags appear in billing reports. They do not require or validate tags."] },

{ id: "d4-22", d: 4,
  q: "A company wants to make sure that S3 buckets in all member accounts can never be accessed by principals outside the organization, even if a bucket owner adds a permissive bucket policy by mistake.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Attach a resource control policy that denies S3 access to principals outside the organization.",
      "Attach a service control policy that denies all S3 actions for principals in the member accounts.",
      "Turn on S3 Block Public Access at the bucket level for each new bucket at the time it is created.",
      "Use IAM Access Analyzer to find buckets that are shared outside of the organization once each week."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Resource control policies (RCPs) set the maximum permissions on resources in member accounts, no matter what their resource-based policies allow. An RCP that denies S3 access when aws:PrincipalOrgID does not match the organization stops external principals even if a bucket policy allows them. Exceptions can be added for AWS service principals.",
      "SCPs limit principals inside the organization. They do not control what external principals can do with your resources, and this SCP would block your own users.",
      "Block Public Access stops public access, but a bucket policy could still grant access to a specific external account.",
      "Weekly detection finds problems after access is already possible. It does not prevent access."] },

{ id: "d4-23", d: 4,
  q: "An administrator edited an SCP that AWS Control Tower created for a governed OU directly in the AWS Organizations console. The Control Tower dashboard now reports drift for the OU.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer resolve the drift?",
  o: ["Re-register the OU in Control Tower, or repair the landing zone, so that the original control is restored.",
      "Delete the Control Tower landing zone, and set up a new landing zone from the beginning in the account.",
      "Edit the drifted SCP again in the Organizations console until the dashboard shows that it is compliant.",
      "Turn off every control on the OU, and then turn them all back on by hand one at a time from Organizations."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Control Tower manages the SCPs for its controls, so changing them outside Control Tower causes drift. Re-registering the OU, or repairing the landing zone, reapplies the expected configuration. Changes to guardrails should be made through Control Tower.",
      "Deleting the landing zone is disruptive and unnecessary for this kind of drift.",
      "Hand edits are what caused the drift, and they might not match what Control Tower expects.",
      "Controls must be managed through Control Tower, not through Organizations, and turning them off removes protection."] },

{ id: "d4-24", d: 4,
  q: "Engineers sign in to AWS through IAM Identity Center. They now need to run AWS CLI commands against several accounts, and they must not create IAM access keys.\n\nWhat should the engineers do?",
  o: ["Configure an AWS CLI profile for IAM Identity Center, and run aws sso login to get temporary credentials.",
      "Create an IAM user with access keys in each account, and add the keys to the AWS CLI configuration file.",
      "Copy the session cookie from the AWS access portal into the AWS CLI configuration on each workstation.",
      "Ask an administrator to share the management account's access keys with all of the engineers' profiles."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The AWS CLI supports IAM Identity Center profiles. aws sso login opens a browser sign-in, and the CLI then gets short-lived credentials for the chosen account and permission set, so no access keys are needed.",
      "This creates long-lived access keys, which the engineers must avoid.",
      "Session cookies are not CLI credentials, and copying them is insecure.",
      "Sharing access keys breaks least privilege and accountability."] },

{ id: "d4-25", d: 4,
  q: "A company wants to require IMDSv2 on all new EC2 instances and to block public sharing of AMIs and EBS snapshots in every account in its organization. The configuration must keep working even when new EC2 APIs or launch paths are introduced.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Attach an EC2 declarative policy to the organization root that sets these account attributes.",
      "Create a Config rule in each account that detects IMDSv1 instances and public AMIs every day.",
      "Write an SCP for every EC2 API that could launch instances or share images, and update it often.",
      "Ask each account administrator to change the EC2 account settings by hand in each Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Declarative policies for EC2 set and enforce the desired configuration of EC2 account attributes across the organization, such as requiring IMDSv2 for new instances and blocking public access to AMIs and snapshots. The setting is enforced by the service itself, so it keeps working as new APIs appear.",
      "Daily detection is reactive and does not prevent the problem.",
      "Many SCP conditions would need constant maintenance as APIs change, which is the overhead the company wants to avoid.",
      "Manual settings in every account and Region are slow, error prone, and easy to undo."] },

{ id: "d4-26", d: 4,
  q: "AWS Trusted Advisor shows a red security check for security groups that allow unrestricted access (0.0.0.0/0) to port 22 on several production instances. Administrators use SSH only for occasional troubleshooting.\n\nWhich remediation will resolve the finding while keeping administrative access?",
  o: ["Remove the SSH rules, and use Systems Manager Session Manager for administrative access.",
      "Change the SSH rules to allow 0.0.0.0/0 on port 2222 instead of port 22 on the instances.",
      "Keep the rules, and turn on GuardDuty so that brute force SSH attempts are detected later.",
      "Keep the rules, and suppress the Trusted Advisor check so that it no longer shows as red."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Session Manager gives shell access through SSM Agent with no inbound ports and no SSH keys, and sessions are controlled by IAM and can be logged. Removing the open SSH rules removes the exposure that Trusted Advisor flagged.",
      "Moving SSH to another port still leaves it open to the whole internet.",
      "GuardDuty detects attacks, but the open port remains exposed.",
      "Hiding the finding does not fix the exposure."] },

{ id: "d4-27", d: 4,
  q: "AWS notifies a company that an IAM user's access key was found in a public GitHub repository. The Trusted Advisor Exposed Access Keys check is also red for the key.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do FIRST?",
  o: ["Deactivate the exposed access key, and replace it with a new key or a role for the workload.",
      "Make the GitHub repository private so that nobody else can see the access key from now on.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail Insights so that unusual API activity from the key is reported in the future.",
      "Add the key to an IAM policy that denies it only for requests that come from outside the VPC."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An exposed key must be treated as compromised. Deactivating it stops further use right away. The team should then replace it, preferably with temporary credentials from a role, review CloudTrail for misuse, and delete the old key.",
      "The key has already been seen and may already be in use by attackers. Hiding the repository does not revoke it.",
      "Detecting unusual activity later does not stop an attacker who already has the key.",
      "IAM policies cannot target a single access key in this way, and the key would still be valid."] },

{ id: "d4-28", d: 4,
  q: "A company wants to be notified, and to start remediation automatically, whenever the status of an AWS Trusted Advisor security check changes to red in its account.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an EventBridge rule for Trusted Advisor check status changes in us-east-1 with SNS and Lambda targets.",
      "Create a CloudWatch alarm that watches the Trusted Advisor console page, and refresh the page every 5 minutes.",
      "Turn on AWS Config in us-east-1, and create a Config rule for each of the Trusted Advisor security checks.",
      "Export the Trusted Advisor report to S3 weekly, and run a Lambda function that compares the reports."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Trusted Advisor sends check status change events to EventBridge in the us-east-1 Region. A rule that matches red security checks can notify the team through Amazon SNS and invoke a Lambda function or an Automation runbook to remediate.",
      "CloudWatch alarms watch metrics, not web pages.",
      "Config rules evaluate resource configurations. They do not receive Trusted Advisor check results.",
      "Weekly comparisons are slow and are custom code to maintain."] },

{ id: "d4-29", d: 4,
  q: "An auditor requires that 40 accounts are continuously evaluated against a common set of controls based on the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, with results for each account. The company uses AWS Organizations.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Deploy an AWS Config conformance pack based on the CIS template to all accounts from the delegated administrator.",
      "Write custom Lambda functions for each CIS control, and run them in every account of the organization once a week.",
      "Run Amazon Inspector in every account, and export the vulnerability findings to the auditor each month.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail in every account, and ask the auditor to read through the trail logs for each of the controls."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A conformance pack is a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions that is deployed as one unit. Sample templates exist for frameworks such as CIS, and organization conformance packs deploy them to all member accounts and report compliance by account.",
      "Custom functions for every control are a lot of code to build and maintain, and weekly runs are not continuous.",
      "Inspector finds software vulnerabilities and network exposure. It does not evaluate CIS account configuration controls.",
      "Logs alone do not evaluate controls. The auditor would have to analyze configurations by hand."] },

{ id: "d4-30", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses AWS Config in 30 accounts and 3 Regions. The compliance team wants a single view of noncompliant resources across all of those accounts and Regions.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Config aggregator for the organization in a central account.",
      "Create a CloudWatch dashboard in each account that shows Config compliance data.",
      "Copy every Config snapshot to one S3 bucket, and read the files by hand each week.",
      "Create a Config rule in the central account that evaluates resources in all accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An aggregator collects AWS Config configuration and compliance data from multiple accounts and Regions, or from a whole organization, into one account. The team can view and query noncompliant resources in one place.",
      "Per-account dashboards do not give a single cross-account view.",
      "Reading snapshot files by hand is slow and does not scale.",
      "Config rules evaluate resources in their own account and Region. An aggregator is needed to combine the results."] },

{ id: "d4-31", d: 4,
  q: "An AWS Config managed rule marks security groups that allow SSH from 0.0.0.0/0 as noncompliant. The security team wants these SSH rules removed automatically within minutes of detection.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Add an automatic remediation action to the Config rule that runs the AWS-DisablePublicAccessForSecurityGroup runbook.",
      "Create a Lambda function that runs every hour, lists every security group in every Region, and removes any open SSH rules.",
      "Turn on GuardDuty so that it removes the open SSH rules when it detects SSH brute force activity.",
      "Email the noncompliant resources to the security team each day so that they can fix them by hand."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Config rules can have remediation actions that run Systems Manager Automation runbooks. With automatic remediation turned on, Config runs the runbook, such as AWS-DisablePublicAccessForSecurityGroup, on each noncompliant security group by using an IAM role that you provide.",
      "A scheduled function is custom code and runs only hourly, so open rules could exist for up to an hour.",
      "GuardDuty detects threats. It does not change security groups by itself.",
      "Manual fixes are slow, and open SSH rules would stay in place for up to a day."] },

{ id: "d4-32", d: 4,
  q: "Company policy requires every EBS volume to have the tag DataClass and to be encrypted. No AWS managed Config rule checks both conditions together, and the team does not want to write or maintain Lambda code.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Config custom policy rule written in CloudFormation Guard.",
      "Create an AWS Config custom Lambda rule written in Python, and maintain its code.",
      "Create a CloudTrail Insights selector for EBS volume events.",
      "Create a Trusted Advisor custom check for the EBS volumes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Custom policy rules use AWS CloudFormation Guard, a policy-as-code language, to evaluate configuration items. They need no Lambda function, and one rule can check that a volume is encrypted and has the DataClass tag.",
      "A Lambda rule works, but the team does not want to write or maintain Lambda code.",
      "CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API activity rates. It does not evaluate resource configurations.",
      "Trusted Advisor does not support custom checks of this kind."] },

{ id: "d4-33", d: 4,
  q: "AWS Config costs have grown because an Auto Scaling fleet launches and terminates thousands of instances each day, and every change is recorded continuously. The compliance team needs only a daily view of the configuration of these resource types.\n\nWhich change will reduce AWS Config costs and still meet the requirement?",
  o: ["Change the recording frequency for those resource types from continuous to daily.",
      "Turn off AWS Config in the account, and use the CloudTrail event history for compliance reports.",
      "Delete all Config rules in the account so that no configuration items are recorded.",
      "Move the delivery channel's S3 bucket to S3 Glacier Deep Archive to reduce cost."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Config supports periodic (daily) recording for selected resource types. Daily recording creates at most one configuration item per resource per day when there are changes, which greatly reduces the cost of short-lived resources.",
      "CloudTrail records API calls, not resource configuration state, and it does not evaluate compliance.",
      "Configuration items are created by the configuration recorder, not by rules, so deleting rules does not stop recording, and it removes the compliance checks.",
      "Most of the cost comes from the number of configuration items recorded, not from storing the delivered files."] },

{ id: "d4-34", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 2,000 S3 buckets across many accounts. The security team needs to find which buckets contain personally identifiable information (PII), such as passport numbers and credit card numbers, and to keep checking as the data changes.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on Amazon Macie with automated sensitive data discovery for the organization.",
      "Turn on Amazon GuardDuty S3 Protection to inspect every object for sensitive data.",
      "Run Amazon Athena queries over every bucket each week to search for PII patterns.",
      "Use S3 Inventory reports to list object names that contain the words passport or card."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Macie uses managed and custom data identifiers to find sensitive data, such as PII and financial data, in Amazon S3. Automated sensitive data discovery samples objects across buckets continuously and keeps a sensitivity score for each bucket, across the organization.",
      "GuardDuty S3 Protection detects suspicious access to S3. It does not inspect object contents for sensitive data.",
      "Athena works only on data that you define tables for, and weekly custom queries across 2,000 buckets are a lot of work.",
      "Object names do not show what the objects contain."] },

{ id: "d4-35", d: 4,
  q: "Amazon Macie is running, and the company also stores internal employee IDs in the format EMP-123456 in Amazon S3. The security team wants Macie to report objects that contain these IDs.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Create a Macie custom data identifier with a regular expression for the ID format.",
      "Create a GuardDuty threat list that contains the employee ID format as a search pattern.",
      "Add the employee ID format to an S3 bucket policy as a condition key for objects.",
      "Create a Config rule that searches object contents for the employee ID format."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Custom data identifiers let Macie detect organization-specific sensitive data by using a regular expression, with optional keywords and proximity rules. Macie then reports objects that contain the employee IDs.",
      "GuardDuty threat lists contain known malicious IP addresses and domains. They are not used to scan data.",
      "Bucket policies control access. They cannot inspect object contents.",
      "Config evaluates resource configurations, not the contents of objects."] },

{ id: "d4-36", d: 4,
  q: "A company is implementing a data classification scheme. Every S3 bucket must be labeled Public, Internal, or Confidential, and only the security team may read Confidential buckets. The company must also detect buckets that have no label.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Tag each bucket with a DataClassification key, and find missing tags with the Config required-tags rule.",
      "Use bucket policies or tag-based conditions that let only the security team's roles read Confidential buckets.",
      "Record each bucket's classification in a spreadsheet, and review it with the security team every quarter.",
      "Encrypt Confidential buckets with SSE-S3 so that only the security team can decrypt the objects in them.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning for Confidential buckets so that other users cannot read the objects in them."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Tags are a common way to label resources with their classification. The AWS Config required-tags managed rule reports buckets that are missing the DataClassification key, so missing labels are found continuously.",
      "Access controls based on the classification, such as a bucket policy or a condition on aws:ResourceTag/DataClassification, make sure that only the security team's roles can read Confidential data. Encryption with a customer managed KMS key adds another layer.",
      "A spreadsheet is not linked to the resources and goes out of date quickly.",
      "S3 manages SSE-S3 keys for every authorized user, so SSE-S3 does not limit who can read the objects.",
      "Versioning keeps object versions. It does not restrict who can read objects."] },

{ id: "d4-37", d: 4,
  q: "An application sometimes writes customer email addresses and credit card numbers into its logs in CloudWatch Logs. The company must mask this data for most users, while a small security team can still see the original values during investigations.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Attach a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy to the log group, and give only the security team logs:Unmask.",
      "Create a metric filter that replaces email addresses and card numbers with asterisks in new log events.",
      "Export the log group to Amazon S3 every day, run a script that deletes the sensitive data, and re-import the logs.",
      "Encrypt the log group with a customer managed KMS key so that the sensitive values are hidden from users."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Data protection policies use managed data identifiers to detect and mask sensitive data, such as email addresses and credit card numbers, as logs are ingested. Users see masked values unless they have the logs:Unmask permission, which can be limited to the security team.",
      "Metric filters count matching events. They do not change log contents.",
      "This is custom work that leaves sensitive data visible until the script runs, and the logs cannot simply be imported back as before.",
      "Encryption protects data at rest. Users who can read the log group still see the plain text values."] },

{ id: "d4-38", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance was created without encryption. A new policy requires all databases to be encrypted at rest with a customer managed KMS key.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Take a snapshot, copy it with encryption using the KMS key, and restore a new DB instance from the copy.",
      "Modify the DB instance, turn on encryption with the KMS key, and apply the change immediately.",
      "Create an encrypted read replica of the unencrypted DB instance, and then promote that read replica to primary.",
      "Turn on default EBS encryption in the Region so that the DB instance's storage becomes encrypted."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Encryption cannot be turned on for an existing unencrypted RDS DB instance. Copying a snapshot with encryption and restoring it creates an encrypted instance. The application then moves to the new endpoint, so the change needs a planned cutover.",
      "RDS does not support turning on encryption for an existing unencrypted DB instance.",
      "RDS cannot create an encrypted read replica from an unencrypted DB instance.",
      "EBS encryption by default applies to EBS volumes that you create. It does not change RDS storage."] },

{ id: "d4-39", d: 4,
  q: "A company wants every new EBS volume in a Region to be encrypted automatically. The company also has an unencrypted data volume, attached to a running instance, that must be encrypted.\n\nWhich combination of actions will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Turn on EBS encryption by default for the account in the Region.",
      "Snapshot the volume, create an encrypted volume from it, and swap the volumes.",
      "Run the modify-volume command with the encrypted option on the existing volume.",
      "Attach an IAM policy that denies the creation of unencrypted EBS volumes only.",
      "Turn on S3 default encryption for the bucket that stores the EBS snapshots."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["EBS encryption by default is a Regional account setting. After it is turned on, all new volumes and snapshot copies in the Region are encrypted with the default KMS key or a key that you choose.",
      "An existing volume cannot be encrypted in place. Creating an encrypted volume from a snapshot, and then attaching it in place of the old volume, encrypts the data. The switch needs a short planned stop for the data volume.",
      "Elastic Volumes can change size, type, and performance, but it cannot encrypt an existing volume.",
      "A deny policy blocks unencrypted volumes, but it does not encrypt anything automatically, and it does nothing for the existing volume.",
      "EBS snapshots are not stored in a bucket that you manage, so S3 bucket settings do not apply."] },

{ id: "d4-40", d: 4,
  q: "A data pipeline writes millions of small objects every hour to an S3 bucket that uses SSE-KMS with a customer managed key. The pipeline receives ThrottlingException errors from AWS KMS, and KMS request costs are high.\n\nWhich solution will reduce the KMS requests with the LEAST effort?",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Bucket Keys for the bucket's SSE-KMS encryption setting.",
      "Switch the bucket to dual-layer encryption with DSSE-KMS for every object.",
      "Create a second KMS key, and split the objects across the two keys at random.",
      "Request a higher limit for the S3 PUT request rate on the bucket's prefixes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With S3 Bucket Keys, S3 uses a short-lived bucket-level key from AWS KMS to create data keys for objects, instead of calling KMS for every object. This can reduce KMS requests and costs by up to 99%.",
      "Dual-layer encryption adds a second layer of encryption and increases KMS use instead of reducing it.",
      "Splitting traffic across keys needs application changes and does not reduce the total number of KMS requests, because request quotas are shared across keys in the account and Region.",
      "The errors come from AWS KMS, not from S3 request rates."] },

{ id: "d4-41", d: 4,
  q: "A compliance standard requires that the key material of a customer managed KMS key is rotated every 180 days. The key was created with key material that AWS KMS generated.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Turn on automatic key rotation for the key with a rotation period of 180 days.",
      "Create a new key every 180 days, and re-encrypt all of the data with the new key by hand.",
      "Turn on automatic rotation for the AWS managed key that the service uses by default.",
      "Schedule the key for deletion every 180 days so that KMS replaces it with a new key."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automatic rotation for customer managed keys supports a custom rotation period from 90 to 2,560 days. KMS creates new key material on that schedule and keeps the older material to decrypt existing data, so nothing needs to be re-encrypted. On-demand rotation is also available.",
      "Manual rotation with re-encryption is a lot of work and is not needed for keys with KMS-generated key material.",
      "AWS managed keys rotate every year on a schedule that you cannot change.",
      "Deleting a key makes all data encrypted under it unrecoverable."] },

{ id: "d4-42", d: 4,
  q: "An application encrypts data in us-east-1 with a KMS key and replicates the encrypted data to eu-west-1 for disaster recovery. The application in eu-west-1 must decrypt the data locally without calling the KMS key in us-east-1.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use a KMS multi-Region primary key in us-east-1 and a replica key in eu-west-1.",
      "Create a new single-Region key in eu-west-1, and use it to decrypt the replicated data.",
      "Export the key material from us-east-1, and import it into the AWS managed key in eu-west-1.",
      "Share the us-east-1 key with eu-west-1 by using AWS RAM so that it works in both Regions."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Multi-Region keys are related keys in different Regions that share the same key ID and key material. Data encrypted with the primary key in us-east-1 can be decrypted with the replica key in eu-west-1 without any cross-Region KMS calls.",
      "A different key cannot decrypt data that was encrypted under the original key.",
      "AWS KMS does not let you export key material that it generated, and AWS managed keys cannot import key material.",
      "KMS keys are not shared across Regions through AWS RAM."] },

{ id: "d4-43", d: 4,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer schedules a customer managed KMS key for deletion with a 30-day waiting period, because the team believes that the key is unused. The team wants to be alerted if anything tries to use the key during the waiting period.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a CloudWatch metric filter and alarm on CloudTrail events for requests that fail because the key is pending deletion.",
      "Turn on automatic key rotation so that the key writes a new entry in CloudTrail each time that it is used.",
      "Disable the key instead, and wait for the users of the key to open a support ticket with the team.",
      "Shorten the waiting period to 7 days so that any problems with the deletion are found sooner after it happens."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["During the waiting period, the key cannot be used, and requests that try to use it fail and are recorded in CloudTrail. A metric filter on those events in CloudWatch Logs, with an alarm, alerts the team so that it can cancel the deletion if the key is still needed.",
      "Rotation creates new key material. It does not alert anyone about attempted use.",
      "Disabling a key first is a safer test, but waiting for tickets is not an alert and may come too late.",
      "A shorter waiting period gives less time to find problems before the key, and any data encrypted under it, is lost for good."] },

{ id: "d4-44", d: 4,
  q: "A bucket's default encryption uses SSE-KMS with a specific customer managed key. Some applications upload objects with an explicit header that requests SSE-S3, which overrides the default. The security team requires every object to be encrypted with that KMS key.\n\nWhich solution will enforce this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that denies PutObject requests whose encryption headers do not specify the required KMS key.",
      "Turn on S3 Bucket Keys so that all objects use the bucket-level key no matter which headers are sent.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock in compliance mode so that objects cannot be uploaded with SSE-S3 encryption.",
      "Rely on the bucket's default encryption setting, because it always overrides any headers that applications send."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A bucket policy can deny PutObject requests unless the encryption headers name SSE-KMS and the required key, by using condition keys such as s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id. Uploads that ask for SSE-S3 or for a different key are rejected.",
      "S3 Bucket Keys reduce KMS request costs. They do not override an explicit request for SSE-S3.",
      "Object Lock protects object versions from deletion. It does not control encryption settings.",
      "Default encryption applies only when a request does not specify encryption. An explicit header overrides it."] },

{ id: "d4-45", d: 4,
  q: "A public website runs behind an Application Load Balancer that has only an HTTP listener on port 80. The company must serve all traffic over HTTPS with a free certificate that renews automatically. Users who type http:// must be sent to HTTPS.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Request a public ACM certificate with DNS validation, and add an HTTPS listener on port 443 that uses it.",
      "Change the port 80 listener's default action to redirect requests to HTTPS on port 443.",
      "Install a self-signed certificate on each EC2 instance, and keep the ALB listener on port 80.",
      "Import a certificate bought from a third-party CA into ACM, and renew it by hand every year.",
      "Add a rule to the ALB security group that blocks inbound port 80 traffic from the internet."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Public ACM certificates are free, and ACM renews DNS-validated certificates automatically as long as the validation CNAME record stays in place. An HTTPS listener on the ALB terminates TLS with the certificate.",
      "A listener rule with a redirect action returns an HTTP 301 response to the HTTPS URL, so users who type http:// are moved to HTTPS.",
      "Browsers do not trust self-signed certificates, and the ALB would still serve plain HTTP to users.",
      "Imported certificates cost money from the CA and are not renewed automatically by ACM.",
      "Security groups cannot block traffic from the internet with a deny rule, and blocking port 80 would break users who type http:// instead of redirecting them."] },

{ id: "d4-46", d: 4,
  q: "ACM sends a notice that a DNS-validated public certificate used by a CloudFront distribution is not eligible for automatic renewal. Someone recently cleaned up old records in the domain's Route 53 hosted zone.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Re-create the ACM validation CNAME record in the hosted zone so that renewal can complete.",
      "Request a new certificate with email validation, and attach it to the distribution each year.",
      "Export the certificate from ACM, and import it again so that the expiration date is extended.",
      "Move the certificate to the Region of the CloudFront origin so that ACM can renew it there."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ACM renews DNS-validated certificates automatically only while the CNAME validation record is present and resolvable. Restoring the record lets ACM validate the domain again and renew the certificate before it expires.",
      "Email validation requires someone to approve each renewal, which adds manual work and risk.",
      "Re-importing does not extend a certificate's expiration date, and ACM does not renew imported certificates.",
      "Certificates for CloudFront must be in us-east-1, regardless of where the origin is."] },

{ id: "d4-47", d: 4,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer requested an ACM certificate for www.example.com in eu-west-1 and wants to use it with a CloudFront distribution. The certificate does not appear in the certificate list in the CloudFront console.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Request or import the certificate in the us-east-1 Region, and select it in CloudFront.",
      "Wait 24 hours for the certificate to replicate from eu-west-1 to all CloudFront locations.",
      "Attach the certificate to an ALB in eu-west-1, and set that ALB as the CloudFront origin.",
      "Share the certificate with the CloudFront service principal by using AWS Resource Access Manager."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront can use only ACM certificates that are in the US East (N. Virginia) Region, us-east-1. The certificate must be requested or imported there.",
      "ACM certificates do not replicate between Regions.",
      "A certificate on the origin load balancer secures traffic from CloudFront to the origin. It does not serve the viewer-facing domain on CloudFront.",
      "ACM certificates are not shared with CloudFront through AWS RAM."] },

{ id: "d4-48", d: 4,
  q: "An auditor finds that clients can download objects from an S3 bucket over plain HTTP. The company requires that all requests to the bucket use TLS.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that denies all S3 actions when aws:SecureTransport is false.",
      "Turn on default encryption with SSE-KMS so that all requests are encrypted in transit.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration so that requests are routed over encrypted connections.",
      "Turn on S3 Block Public Access so that HTTP requests from the internet are rejected."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["aws:SecureTransport is false for requests made over HTTP. A Deny statement with that condition rejects every unencrypted request, so clients must use HTTPS.",
      "Server-side encryption protects data at rest. It does not stop requests from using plain HTTP.",
      "Transfer Acceleration speeds up transfers. It does not block HTTP requests.",
      "Block Public Access controls public permissions. It does not decide whether authorized requests use TLS."] },

{ id: "d4-49", d: 4,
  q: "A company requires that every connection to its Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance uses TLS. Some older clients still connect without TLS.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer enforce this requirement?",
  o: ["Set the rds.force_ssl parameter to 1 in the DB parameter group, and update the clients.",
      "Turn on encryption at rest for the DB instance by restoring it from an encrypted snapshot copy.",
      "Add a security group rule that allows only port 443 to the DB instance from the application.",
      "Turn on IAM database authentication so that all connections are encrypted automatically."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The rds.force_ssl parameter makes the database reject connections that do not use SSL/TLS. Clients must be updated to connect with TLS and to trust the RDS certificate authority before the change, or they will fail to connect.",
      "Encryption at rest protects stored data. It does not force connections to use TLS.",
      "PostgreSQL listens on its own port, usually 5432. A port 443 rule does not control whether database connections use TLS.",
      "IAM authentication changes how users authenticate. It does not force other clients to use TLS."] },

{ id: "d4-50", d: 4,
  q: "A company imports certificates from a third-party certificate authority into ACM for several load balancers. ACM does not renew imported certificates, and one of them expired last month and caused an outage.\n\nWhich solution will warn the team at least 30 days before an imported certificate expires?",
  o: ["Create a CloudWatch alarm on the ACM DaysToExpiry metric, or an EventBridge rule for expiration events.",
      "Turn on automatic renewal for each imported certificate in the ACM console settings.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events for ACM so that expirations are logged in the trail.",
      "Create a Route 53 health check for each of the domains so that it fails on the day that the certificate expires."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ACM publishes the DaysToExpiry metric for certificates and sends approaching expiration events to EventBridge, starting 45 days before expiration by default. An alarm or rule can notify the team in time to renew the certificate and import it again. The acm-certificate-expiration-check Config rule is another option.",
      "ACM cannot renew imported certificates. They must be renewed with the issuing CA and imported again.",
      "Expiration is not an API call, so CloudTrail does not record it.",
      "A health check that fails on the expiration day gives no warning in advance."] },

{ id: "d4-51", d: 4,
  q: "A security standard requires that an Application Load Balancer accepts only TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 with strong ciphers. A scan shows that the HTTPS listener still accepts TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Change the HTTPS listener's security policy to one that allows only TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3.",
      "Import a new certificate into ACM that was issued with the TLS 1.3 protocol turned on for it.",
      "Add a security group rule that blocks clients that try to connect with TLS 1.0 or 1.1.",
      "Turn on AWS WAF with a rule that blocks TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 requests at the listener."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The listener's security policy, such as ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06, sets which TLS versions and ciphers the load balancer negotiates. Choosing a policy that allows only TLS 1.2 and 1.3 stops the older protocols.",
      "Certificates are not tied to a TLS version. The protocol versions come from the listener's security policy.",
      "Security groups filter by IP address, port, and protocol. They cannot see TLS versions.",
      "AWS WAF inspects HTTP requests after TLS is negotiated, so it cannot control which TLS versions the listener accepts."] },

{ id: "d4-52", d: 4,
  q: "An application keeps the master password for an Amazon RDS for MySQL database in a configuration file. The company requires that the password is stored securely and rotated every 30 days without custom code.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Let RDS manage the master user password in Secrets Manager, and set the rotation schedule to 30 days.",
      "Store the password as a SecureString in Parameter Store, and turn on its automatic rotation feature.",
      "Store the password in an encrypted S3 object, and replace it every 30 days by using a scheduled Lambda script.",
      "Store the password in a Lambda environment variable, and update the variable by hand each month."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When RDS manages the master user password, it stores the password in Secrets Manager and rotates it with managed rotation, with no Lambda function to write. The application reads the current value from Secrets Manager. The default rotation period is 7 days, and it can be changed.",
      "Parameter Store does not rotate SecureString parameters automatically.",
      "This is custom code, and the database password would still have to be changed and kept in sync correctly.",
      "Manual updates are error prone, and environment variables are not a secure secret store."] },

{ id: "d4-53", d: 4,
  q: "A Secrets Manager rotation function runs in private subnets so that it can reach an Amazon RDS database. Rotation fails, and the function's logs show timeouts when it calls the Secrets Manager API. The subnets have no NAT gateway.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Create an interface VPC endpoint for Secrets Manager that allows HTTPS from the function's security group.",
      "Move the rotation function out of the VPC so that it can reach both Secrets Manager and the database.",
      "Increase the rotation function's timeout from 30 seconds to 15 minutes so that the API call can finish.",
      "Attach the SecretsManagerReadWrite policy to the database's IAM role so that rotation can complete."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A function in private subnets without a NAT gateway has no path to the public Secrets Manager endpoint. An interface VPC endpoint gives it a private path, and the endpoint's security group must allow inbound HTTPS from the function.",
      "Outside the VPC, the function could reach Secrets Manager but not the private database.",
      "The calls time out because there is no network path, so a longer timeout only delays the failure.",
      "This is a network problem, not a permissions problem, and the database does not call Secrets Manager."] },

{ id: "d4-54", d: 4,
  q: "A secret in account A stores API credentials. An application role in account B must read the secret. The secret is encrypted with the AWS managed key aws/secretsmanager.\n\nWhich solution will allow the role in account B to read the secret?",
  o: ["Encrypt the secret with a customer managed key, and allow account B in the key policy and the secret policy.",
      "Add a resource policy to the secret that allows account B to read it, and keep using the aws/secretsmanager key.",
      "Share the secret with account B by using AWS RAM, and keep using the aws/secretsmanager key for it.",
      "Replicate the secret to account B by using Secrets Manager replication to a Region in that account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-account access to a secret needs a resource policy on the secret that allows the role, a customer managed KMS key whose key policy allows account B to decrypt, and an IAM policy in account B that allows GetSecretValue and kms:Decrypt. The policy of the AWS managed key cannot be changed to allow another account.",
      "The resource policy alone is not enough, because account B cannot use the AWS managed key.",
      "Secrets Manager uses resource policies, not AWS RAM, for cross-account access, and account B still could not use the AWS managed key.",
      "Replication copies a secret to other Regions in the same account, not to another account."] },

{ id: "d4-55", d: 4,
  q: "A multi-Region application reads database credentials from Secrets Manager in us-east-1. During a disaster recovery test in us-west-2, the application cannot start because it cannot reach the secret.\n\nWhich solution will make the secret available in us-west-2 and keep it in sync with rotations?",
  o: ["Replicate the secret to us-west-2 with Secrets Manager multi-Region secret replication.",
      "Create a new secret in us-west-2 by hand, and copy the value each time the secret rotates.",
      "Store a copy of the secret value in Parameter Store in us-west-2 as a String parameter.",
      "Grant the us-west-2 application access to the us-east-1 secret's KMS key in that Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Secrets Manager can replicate a secret to other Regions. Replicas stay in sync when the primary secret's value changes, including through rotation, and the application can read the replica locally during a Regional outage.",
      "Manual copies drift out of sync after each rotation.",
      "A String parameter stores the credentials in plain text, and it does not stay in sync with rotations.",
      "The secret itself would still exist only in us-east-1, which might be unavailable during a disaster."] },

{ id: "d4-56", d: 4,
  q: "An application needs a third-party license key that never rotates, along with 30 other configuration values. The team wants the license key encrypted at rest at the lowest cost.\n\nWhere should a CloudOps engineer store the license key?",
  o: ["In Parameter Store as a SecureString standard parameter",
      "In Secrets Manager with a 30-day automatic rotation schedule",
      "In a plaintext String parameter in Parameter Store",
      "In the application's source code repository"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["SecureString parameters are encrypted with AWS KMS, and standard parameters have no storage charge. They suit secrets that do not need automatic rotation, such as a fixed license key.",
      "Secrets Manager charges for each secret every month and is built for rotation, which this key does not need.",
      "A String parameter is not encrypted, so the license key would be stored in plain text.",
      "Secrets in source code can leak through the repository and its history."] },

{ id: "d4-57", d: 4,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty reports a CryptoCurrency:EC2/BitcoinTool.B!DNS finding for an EC2 instance. For future findings of this type, the security team wants the instance isolated automatically and its disks preserved for forensics.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use an EventBridge rule on the finding type to start automation that isolates the instance and snapshots its volumes.",
      "Turn on GuardDuty Malware Protection for EC2 so that GuardDuty terminates the instance as soon as it finds malware.",
      "Create a suppression rule for the finding type so that the instance stops sending cryptocurrency DNS queries.",
      "Add a GuardDuty trusted IP list that contains the instance's IP address so that its traffic is blocked."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty sends findings to EventBridge. A rule that matches the finding type can start a Systems Manager Automation runbook or a Lambda function that replaces the instance's security groups with an isolation security group and creates EBS snapshots for forensics.",
      "Malware Protection scans EBS volumes and reports findings. It does not terminate instances, and terminating would destroy evidence.",
      "Suppression rules hide findings from view. They do not stop the activity, and they would hide a real threat.",
      "Trusted IP lists tell GuardDuty not to generate findings for those addresses. They do not block traffic."] },

{ id: "d4-58", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs an approved vulnerability scanner from a fixed EC2 instance. Every day, GuardDuty generates many Recon:EC2/Portscan findings for this scanner, which hides real findings.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Create a GuardDuty suppression rule that matches the finding type and the scanner instance.",
      "Turn off GuardDuty in the account during the hours of each day when the scanner is running.",
      "Archive each Portscan finding by hand from the GuardDuty console after the security team reviews it.",
      "Add the scanner's IP address to a GuardDuty threat list so that its findings are grouped together."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Suppression rules automatically archive new findings that match criteria such as the finding type and the instance ID. The expected scanner findings no longer appear in the active list, while other findings are unaffected.",
      "Turning off GuardDuty leaves the account unmonitored for real threats during those hours.",
      "Archiving findings by hand every day is repetitive work that suppression rules automate.",
      "Threat lists contain known malicious IP addresses and would generate more findings, not fewer."] },

{ id: "d4-59", d: 4,
  q: "Users upload files to an S3 bucket through a public web application. The company wants every new object scanned for malware automatically and infected files tagged so that downstream processing skips them. The company does not want to manage scanning servers.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 for the bucket with object tagging turned on.",
      "Turn on Amazon Macie automated discovery for the bucket to find and tag infected objects.",
      "Run an antivirus agent on EC2 instances that download and scan each new object from the bucket.",
      "Turn on Amazon Inspector for the bucket so that new objects are scanned for vulnerabilities."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 scans newly uploaded objects in the buckets that you choose, with no infrastructure to manage. It can tag each object with the scan result, so downstream processes or bucket policies can block infected files.",
      "Macie finds sensitive data such as PII. It does not scan for malware.",
      "Scanning servers must be built, patched, and scaled, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "Inspector scans EC2 instances, container images, and Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities, not S3 objects for malware."] },

{ id: "d4-60", d: 4,
  q: "Amazon Inspector is turned on, but several EC2 instances show as not covered for vulnerability scanning. The instances do not have SSM Agent, and the company does not want to install agents on them.\n\nHow can a CloudOps engineer get vulnerability findings for these instances?",
  o: ["Turn on agentless scanning in Inspector so that it scans snapshots of the instances' EBS volumes.",
      "Turn on GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring so that Inspector receives vulnerability data from it.",
      "Turn on AWS Config recording for the instances so that Inspector can read their package list.",
      "Turn on VPC Flow Logs for the instances so that Inspector can find their open ports and their old packages."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Inspector's hybrid scan mode can scan EC2 instances that do not have SSM Agent by taking snapshots of their EBS volumes and analyzing the installed software. Instances that have the agent keep agent-based scanning.",
      "Runtime Monitoring detects threats in running workloads for GuardDuty. It does not supply package inventories to Inspector.",
      "Inspector does not use Config data to find software vulnerabilities.",
      "Flow logs record network traffic metadata. They do not show installed software versions."] },

{ id: "d4-61", d: 4,
  q: "The security team wants all accounts in the organization evaluated against the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard. The same standards and controls must be turned on in every account and Region, and they must be managed from one place.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Use Security Hub CSPM central configuration from the delegated administrator with a configuration policy.",
      "Turn on Security Hub CSPM in each account and Region by hand, and enable the standard in each one.",
      "Deploy a separate AWS Config rule for each control in the standard by using a StackSet for every account.",
      "Turn on Amazon Inspector in every account so that it checks the controls in the best practices standard."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Central configuration lets the delegated administrator create configuration policies that turn on Security Hub CSPM, standards, and controls for chosen accounts, OUs, and Regions. New accounts get the same settings automatically.",
      "Configuring every account and Region by hand is slow and drifts over time.",
      "Rebuilding the standard as separate Config rules is a large effort that Security Hub CSPM already provides.",
      "Inspector finds software vulnerabilities. It does not evaluate the best practices controls."] },

{ id: "d4-62", d: 4,
  q: "Security Hub CSPM generates findings for a control whose risk the company has accepted for its development accounts only. The team wants these findings marked as suppressed automatically in development accounts, while the same findings stay active in production accounts.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Create an automation rule that matches the control in development accounts and sets the status to SUPPRESSED.",
      "Disable the control in every account and Region in the organization, including the production accounts.",
      "Archive each finding in the development accounts by hand after it appears in the Security Hub console.",
      "Turn off Security Hub CSPM in the development accounts so that the findings are no longer created."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automation rules update findings as Security Hub receives them, based on criteria such as the control ID and the account ID. Setting the workflow status to SUPPRESSED for development accounts hides the accepted findings there, while production findings stay active.",
      "Disabling the control everywhere removes the check from production too.",
      "Manual handling is repetitive and easy to forget.",
      "Turning off Security Hub CSPM removes all of the other checks in those accounts."] },

{ id: "d4-63", d: 4,
  q: "A security team receives separate findings from Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, Amazon Macie, and Security Hub CSPM. The team wants the signals correlated to show which resources face the highest risk, such as an internet-reachable instance with a critical vulnerability and access to sensitive data.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST effort?",
  o: ["Turn on AWS Security Hub, which correlates the signals into exposures and prioritizes risks.",
      "Export all findings to Amazon S3, and write Athena queries that join them by resource ID.",
      "Turn on AWS Config aggregators so that all findings are combined into compliance scores.",
      "Create a CloudWatch dashboard that shows the finding counts for each service side by side."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Security Hub correlates signals from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, and Security Hub CSPM and identifies exposures, such as a reachable resource with an exploitable vulnerability. It prioritizes risks so that the team can act on the most important issues first.",
      "Custom queries can join findings, but building and maintaining risk correlation logic is a large effort.",
      "Config aggregators combine configuration and compliance data. They do not correlate security findings from these services.",
      "Counts side by side do not show which specific resources combine several risks."] },

{ id: "d4-64", d: 4,
  q: "A company wants to test a new customer-facing web application for exploitable vulnerabilities before each major release. The company has no penetration testing staff and wants on-demand tests that take the application's design and code into account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use AWS Security Agent to run on-demand penetration tests against the application.",
      "Turn on Amazon GuardDuty for the account so that it tests the application for attacks.",
      "Turn on Amazon Inspector so that it performs penetration tests on the web application.",
      "Turn on AWS Shield Advanced so that it simulates attacks against the application."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Security Agent performs on-demand penetration testing that uses context from the application's design documents, source code, and infrastructure as code. It reports exploitable vulnerabilities with guidance for fixing them, and it also offers design and code security reviews.",
      "GuardDuty detects threats from activity in the account. It does not attack your application to find vulnerabilities.",
      "Inspector scans for known software vulnerabilities and network reachability. It does not perform penetration tests of application logic.",
      "Shield Advanced protects against DDoS attacks. It does not test applications for vulnerabilities."] },

{ id: "d4-65", d: 4,
  q: "Administrators use Session Manager to access EC2 instances. Auditors require a record of every command typed in each session, and the session data must be encrypted with a key that the company controls.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Configure Session Manager preferences to send session logs to S3 or CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Turn on KMS encryption of session data with a customer managed key in the preferences.",
      "Open port 22 on the instances so that session commands can be captured by VPC Flow Logs.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events so that every command typed in a session is recorded.",
      "Install the CloudWatch agent so that it captures the keyboard input of each session."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Session Manager can upload or stream session output, including the commands that are run, to an S3 bucket or a CloudWatch Logs log group for auditing.",
      "Session Manager preferences can require that session data is encrypted with a customer managed KMS key, in addition to the TLS encryption that protects data in transit.",
      "Session Manager does not use SSH, and flow logs record only traffic metadata, not commands.",
      "CloudTrail records the StartSession API call and who started it, but not the commands typed inside the session.",
      "The CloudWatch agent collects metrics and log files. It is not the way to record session activity."] },
]);
